  "stage": "synthesis-review",
  "summary": "PASS for the First Sunday of Advent, Year B, concise study. Its own commentary explains how grace enables a person to ask for help and how Christ\u2019s gifts equip servants for duties they must answer for. I checked those distinctions against the expansive study, Jerome on Isaiah XVII, Bede on Mark 13:33\u201337 and Chrysostom\u2019s Homily 2 on First Corinthians. At Communion, Augustine\u2019s Exposition on Psalm 84 \u00a712 supports the preacher sowing, God sending the rain and the former thief delighting in giving, while Bellarmine\u2019s explanation 12 identifies the fruit with Christ born of Mary. The revised liturgical note accurately limits the Offertory statement to the U.S. Missal (p. 139) and distinguishes the seasonal response from the common sung psalms (GIRM 61, 74 and Lectionary 173\u2013174). In the converted reader, the Mark 13:33\u201337 row locates Jesus\u2019 charge to watch on Mount Olivet opposite the Temple, c. A.D. 29 in Maas\u2019s disputed Passion chronology. Separate rows locate composition traditionally at Rome or possibly in Syria. No print export was requested. Both standing blocking findings and the advisory are resolved."
